# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# tools/benchmark_three_way.py / tools/generate_benchmark_report.py output:
# hardware-specific, point-in-time generated artifacts, not source -- the
# tooling that produces them is what's committed (see docs/ROADMAP.md).
benchmark-results/

# benchmarks/aws/runner/aws_benchmark_runner.py + reporting/* output: same
# reasoning as benchmark-results/ above -- real, point-in-time numbers for
# one specific run (real AWS costs and rates drift over time), not source.
# The tooling that produces them is committed; docs/COST_ESTIMATES.md is
# where the real headline numbers from an actual run get recorded in
# prose/table form once one has happened, so they're not lost even though
# the raw JSON/CSV isn't tracked. Each run's full output (results.json,
# cost.json, aggregated.json, report/, dashboard.html, report.pdf) lives
# under its own runs/<timestamp>-sf<N>/ directory -- see docs/RUNBOOK.md.
benchmarks/aws/runs/

# Terraform (benchmarks/aws/terraform/): state can contain resource
# attributes that shouldn't be public (and is single-environment/
# point-in-time, not source), .terraform/ is a large local provider-plugin
# cache, tfplan is a binary artifact, and terraform.tfvars holds
# deployer-specific values (e.g. allowed_ssh_cidr -- a real IP) that don't
# belong in git history. terraform.tfvars.example (if added) is meant to
# be committed; this only excludes the real, filled-in file.
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
tfplan
terraform.tfvars
!terraform.tfvars.example
